  "notes": "The priest's own prayer after the dismissal, said bowed over the middle of the altar and said secretly: he asks that the service he has just rendered be acceptable, and that the sacrifice he offered unworthily be propitiatory for himself and for all he offered it for. ÓCULIS at w013 is dative with ÓBTULI at w017: the sacrifice is presented to the eyes of the divine majesty, idiomatically in its sight. MISERÁNTE at w030 is the present participle of the deponent miseror, active in form as every deponent's present participle is; te miseránte is an ablative absolute. PROPITIÁBILE at w031 is confirmed by Collatinus alone: the word is rare — attested as early as Ennius — and Whitaker's lexicon does not carry it, so the provenance names only the analyzer that does. MISERÁNTE at w030 is feminine: the ablative absolute's te is the sancta Trínitas addressed, and the corpus tags the agreement as the antecedent shows it. ÓBTULI at w017 and w027 carry no conj: offero is a compound of fero and irregular.",
